in this video we're going to talk a  little bit about  ionic compounds and specifically  we're going to think about the empirical  formulas for ionic compounds  so the first thing you might be  wondering is well refreshed me sal and  what ionic compound is  well we're going to be dealing with ions  which you can view as the charged  versions  of atoms so if you start with a neutral  atom the only way it gets charged is if  you are gaining or  losing electrons and that would make it  an ion and a compound  that tells us that we're dealing with  more than one type of  element so let's just get a little bit  of a refresher here  so we talk about this in depth in other  videos but we know that atoms  like to have a full outer shell of  electrons a full number of  valence electrons now in most  situations that means having eight  valence electrons  and that's why these noble gases right  over here are considered to be so stable  they're already very satisfied they have  eight  valence electrons they all have  different numbers of electrons but that  outer shell  is eight and the one exception is when  we're talking about that first shell and  once again this is all review that we  talk about in other videos  that first shell is most stable when it  has two electrons and that's why helium  is  also so stable so in general when  atoms react they tend to react in a way  that gets them  to either two electrons in their first  shell or eight electrons in any other  outer shell whichever one is their  outermost shell  now how they get that they can do that  in several different ways and it really  depends on whether we're talking about  metals or non-metals  so first let me separate the non-metals  here so hydrogen is a non-metal  and then we'll come here and this little  staircase looking thing  so everything to the right and up of  this dividing line that i just drew  these are all non-metals including  hydrogen now when non-metals interact to  try to get to that full  outer shell they tend to do it by  sharing electrons with each other  for example two hydrogens each of which  have one  outer electron well they can form a  covalent bond which we talk about in  other videos  and then they're each sharing each  other's electrons so they can kind of  pretend  like they have a full outer shell now  it's different when we're talking about  a non-metal  interacting with a metal which we can  generally refer to the rest of the  periodic table there's other further  subclassifications  when you have a metal interacting with a  non-metal  what typically happens is that the  non-metal is actually going to  gain electrons to get to a full outer  shell  while the metal will lose electrons to  get to a full outer shell  so i'll give you an example and i use  this example a lot and this is a common  example that gets us to table salt  if you look at sodium right over here a  neutral sodium atom  has one valence electron one electron in  that third  shell well the easiest way for it to get  to a full outer shell is to give up that  one electron  and then it will have the same number of  electrons as neon  it still has 11 protons which make it a  sodium it'll have the same number of  electrons as  neon and then it will be happy so sodium  tends to form a sodium ion  with a positive one charge  now we can go on the other side of the  periodic table if we were to look at  chlorine right over here  chlorine has seven valence electrons it  has seven electrons in that third  shell well the easiest way for it to get  a full eight  is to nab an electron from some places  to gain an electron  so chlorine tends to take electrons from  other atoms and forms a negative  charge and becomes the chloride anion  now what do you think will happen if you  put a bunch of sodium and a bunch of  chlorine  together well the chlorine is probably  going to take electrons from the sodium  or the sodium is likely to give  electrons to the chlorine  and then you have these two ions one is  positively charged one is negatively  charged  they will be attracted to each other and  that's what forms the ionic compound  sodium chloride and this is the  empirical formula for table salt for  sodium chloride for every sodium  ion you have a chloride ion it's  interesting to note that even though the  sodium has a positive charge  the chlorine which is now the chloride  anion has a negative charge  that the ionic compound in total  has a neutral charge which is an  interesting property of  these ionic compounds
 

